---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/ui/CodeBlock.astro';

const conflictRecoveryExample = `// Optimistic revision conflict payload example (409 Conflict)
{
  "error": {
    "code": "REVISION_CONFLICT",
    "message": "The active locale has been updated by another editor since your session began.",
    "currentRevision": 14,
    "expectedRevision": 12
  }
}`;
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Publishing & Staging Workflows"
  description="Understanding Direct Publishing vs Draft Staging modes, optimistic concurrency control, and revision management."
>
  <h1>Publishing & Staging Workflows</h1>

  <p class="lead">
    CopyPatch provides two distinct operational workflows to accommodate different editorial models: <strong>Direct Mode</strong> for instantaneous live updates and <strong>Draft Mode</strong> for review and staging approval cycles.
  </p>

  <h2>Mode Comparison</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Feature</th>
        <th>Direct Mode (Default)</th>
        <th>Draft Mode (Staging)</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Primary Action</strong></td>
        <td><code>Save</code> commits instantly to live snapshot.</td>
        <td><code>Save Draft</code> stages changes into isolated draft state.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Visitor Visibility</strong></td>
        <td>Immediate upon saving.</td>
        <td>Hidden until explicitly promoted via <code>Publish</code>.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Best For</strong></td>
        <td>Fast-moving product teams, typo fixes, founders.</td>
        <td>Agencies, multi-stakeholder reviews, scheduled launches.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Server Flag</strong></td>
        <td><code>--mode direct</code></td>
        <td><code>--mode draft</code></td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>1. Direct Mode Workflow</h2>
  <p>
    In Direct Mode, clicking <strong>Save</strong> (or pressing <code>Cmd+S</code> / <code>Ctrl+S</code>) atomically updates the published snapshot in SQLite and swaps the in-memory cache:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>Editor navigates to <code>?copypatch=1</code> and enters passphrase.</li>
    <li>Editor clicks any text element and types modifications inline.</li>
    <li>Editor clicks <strong>Save</strong> in the floating toolbar.</li>
    <li>Backend commits changes within a SQLite transaction and increments <code>publishedRevision</code>.</li>
    <li>All public visitors immediately receive updated copy on their next page load or client navigation.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>2. Draft Mode Workflow (Review & Stage)</h2>
  <p>
    In Draft Mode, changes remain isolated from public visitors until explicitly approved:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>Editor makes inline adjustments and clicks <strong>Save Draft</strong>.</li>
    <li>The draft text is stored in SQLite under <code>draftRevision</code> without altering the live visitor snapshot.</li>
    <li>Stakeholders and clients inspect the live staging environment with <code>?copypatch=1</code> to review staged modifications in real context.</li>
    <li>Once approved, the authorized editor clicks <strong>Publish</strong> in the toolbar.</li>
    <li>The backend atomically promotes all draft entries to the published snapshot in a single SQLite transaction and increments <code>publishedRevision</code>.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>3. Optimistic Concurrency & Conflict Prevention</h2>
  <p>
    To prevent concurrent editors from accidentally overwriting each other's edits, CopyPatch enforces optimistic concurrency control:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Every mutation request transmits <code>expectedPublishedRevision</code> and <code>expectedDraftRevision</code>.</li>
    <li>If another editor published changes while your session was open, the server rejects the request with HTTP <code>409 Conflict (REVISION_CONFLICT)</code>.</li>
    <li>The client UI prompts the editor to refresh the snapshot, inspect diffs, and reapply changes safely without data loss.</li>
  </ul>

  <CodeBlock code={conflictRecoveryExample} lang="json" title="Conflict Error Response" />

  <h2>4. Revision History & Audit Trails</h2>
  <p>
    Every publish action records an immutable row in the <code>revisions</code> audit table, tracking:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Target locale (e.g. <code>"en"</code>, <code>"tr"</code>)</li>
    <li>Previous and new revision integer numbers</li>
    <li>List of modified content keys and previous values</li>
    <li>Cryptographic session identifier and timestamp</li>
  </ul>
</DocsLayout>
